hat matters most. Press the buttons to see the cells of F and of G: F's is much bigger. (The cells are shaded by how many MU they give.)","s":{"tA":"","tB":"","mF":"","mG":""}},{"c":"This is the simple example again. F uses the big cell 80c8c, made of four quarters. One quarter, 80c8d, holds Las Vegas. The other three are quiet desert and lake. G is small enough to use the Las Vegas quarter on its own.","s":{"zq:80c8d":"cell12","zq:80c89":"cell","zq:80c8b":"cell","zq:80c8f":"cell","@zt":"t"}},{"c":"The four quarters have very different densities. Las Vegas has 619 MU per km², the others only 17, 36, 75. The big cell has the mean of the four: 187.","s":{"@zt":"","@zd":"t"},"eq":"(619.1 + 16.9 + 36.4 + 75.4) / 4 = 187.0"},{"c":"F is big, so it has to use the whole big cell. It counts 2,223 km² of it at 187. That one cell gives 415,684 of the 425,012 MU of F, because the city is averaged with the desert.","s":{},"eq":"F: 2,223 km² × 187 = 415,684"},{"c":"G is small enough to use the Las Vegas quarter alone. It counts 1,190 km² of it at 619. That is 3.3 times the density that F uses for the same ground.","s":{},"eq":"G: 1,190 km² × 619 = 736,490"},{"c":"So G, which lies inside F, earns more: 846,372 MU against 425,012. Bigger fields have to use bigger cells, and a big cell averages a city with its empty surroundings. Press the buttons to compare the two coverings.","s":{"@zq":"","@zd":"","mF":"b","mG":"b"},"tb":["totals",2,null],"eq":"MU(G) = 846,372  >  MU(F) = 425,012"}],"place":"Las Vegas / Lake Mead","bins":{"F:80c8c":9,"F:80c917":0,"F:80c91c":0,"F:80c93":5,"F:80c95":3,"F:80cb8ab":0,"F:80cbf4":0,"F:80cbfc":0,"F:80cc04":4,"F:80cc0c":0,"F:80cc74":0,"F:80cc79c":0,"F:80ceb":1,"F:80ced":6,"F:80cf3":4,"F:80cf44":3,"F:80cf4c":4,"G:80c88b9":0,"G:80c88d":2,"G:80c893":4,"G:80c895":7,"G:80c8b4":3,"G:80c8bc":7,"G:80c8d":9,"G:80c8e4":3,"G:80c8ec":8,"G:80c8f3":0,"G:80c924":1,"G:80c92c":4,"G:80cf2c":1,"G:80cf34":4,"G:80cf39":2,"G:80cf44":2,"G:80cf4c":4},"ramp":{"label":"Contributi